The output you are seeing when you print an array like this:
[I@4c203ea1
is not the actual memory address of the array, but rather a string representation of the array object, generated by the default toString() method of Java's Object class.

Here’s how to interpret the output:

[I: This part indicates that the object is a one-dimensional array of integers (int).

The [I stands for "array of integers":
[ means it's an array.
I means the type is int.
@4c203ea1: This part is the hash code of the array object, represented in hexadecimal form.

This is not the memory address of the array, but rather the hash code that Java generates for the object by calling its hashCode() method. The hashCode() is often loosely related to the memory address but is not the actual address.
Is This the Address of the First Byte?
No, this is not the actual memory address of the first byte (or any byte) of the array in the heap. Java does not provide a direct way to access memory addresses due to its abstraction and security features like memory safety and garbage collection. The value you see (4c203ea1) is a hash code, not the physical memory address.

Why This Happens
In Java, arrays do not override the toString() method from Object, so when you try to print an array directly, the default toString() method is called.
The default behavior of toString() for an object prints the class type followed by the "@" symbol and then the hash code in hexadecimal.
How to Print Array Contents
If you want to print the actual elements of the array, you need to use Arrays.toString() from the java.util.Arrays class, like this:


import java.util.Arrays;

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a));

This will give you a human-readable format, showing the actual contents of the array. For example, if your array was initialized to 0s, you would get:
[0, 0, 0, 0]
Summary:
The output [I@4c203ea1 is a class type representation of the array object along with its hash code.
It is not the actual memory address of the array or its elements.
If you want to print the contents of the array, use Arrays.toString(a).

